WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.271 --> 00:00:05.700
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:11.263
Imagine, imagine that you're seeing
things that aren't there and you're

00:00:11.263 --> 00:00:17.013
confused about time and place and you
quickly forget things that happen to you.

00:00:17.013 --> 00:00:22.368
You're hallucinating, you're delirious,
you're suffering from amnesia,

00:00:22.368 --> 00:00:26.945
if you were to walk into a doctor's
office with any of these symptoms,

00:00:26.945 --> 00:00:29.290
you'd be declared psychopathic.

00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:33.438
Maybe even placed in an institution,
and yet

00:00:33.438 --> 00:00:39.129
all of us were in this state 12 hours ago,
we were sleeping.

00:00:39.129 --> 00:00:40.943
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:00:40.943 --> 00:00:43.104
that's the seeming paradox of sleep.

00:00:43.104 --> 00:00:46.153
It's the state to what's
selected by nature,

00:00:46.153 --> 00:00:50.129
with a course of 3.6 million years,
and it makes no sense.

00:00:50.129 --> 00:00:53.745
Think about it from an evolutionary
perspective, when you're sleeping,

00:00:53.745 --> 00:00:56.741
you're not finding food,
you're clearly not reproducing,

00:00:56.741 --> 00:00:58.672
you're not even fending for yourself.

00:00:58.672 --> 00:01:04.088
You're totally vulnerable on any of
these grounds sleep should have been

00:01:04.088 --> 00:01:10.376
strongly selected against, and yet here we
are three and a half million years later,

00:01:10.376 --> 00:01:15.285
spending a third of our lives in
this state of flagrant psychosis.

00:01:15.285 --> 00:01:18.899
If sleep doesn't serve
some truly vital function,

00:01:18.899 --> 00:01:23.670
then it's the biggest mistake
the evolutionary process ever made.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:27.374
We now know that sleep does have
many vital functions from cementing,

00:01:27.374 --> 00:01:30.767
learning and consolidating memory,
to regulating emotion and

00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:33.748
fostering the association
of disparate experiences.

00:01:33.748 --> 00:01:38.407
And we also know that the vast majority
of us need between seven to nine hours of

00:01:38.407 --> 00:01:40.784
sleep per night to function properly.

00:01:40.784 --> 00:01:46.546
So why do we have such a hard time getting
the amount of sleep our brains so crave?

00:01:46.546 --> 00:01:51.636
Well, because we live in a culture
that prices sleep machismo,

00:01:51.636 --> 00:01:54.571
and we perpetuate a series of myths.

00:01:54.571 --> 00:01:56.553
So let's talk about a couple of them.

00:01:56.553 --> 00:02:00.393
So first myth, you might say,
I can go with five or

00:02:00.393 --> 00:02:06.533
six hours of sleep per night and feel
just fine, and you'd be partially right.

00:02:06.533 --> 00:02:10.965
There are some people that have a rare
genetic mutation that allows them to get

00:02:10.965 --> 00:02:12.124
by with five hours or

00:02:12.124 --> 00:02:15.952
less of sleep each night without
showing any signs of impairment.

00:02:15.952 --> 00:02:21.021
However, according to Dr. Thomas Ruth
from the Henry Ford Hospital in Detroit,

00:02:21.021 --> 00:02:24.767
the proportion of the population for
which this is the case,

00:02:24.767 --> 00:02:27.719
rounded to the nearest whole number,
is zero.

00:02:27.719 --> 00:02:31.229
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's negligible, for

00:02:31.229 --> 00:02:35.376
the rest of us insufficient sleep
can have serious consequences.

00:02:35.376 --> 00:02:37.399
So let me tell you about an experiment.

00:02:37.399 --> 00:02:41.898
Dr. David Dinges, the University of
Pennsylvania took two groups of people.

00:02:41.898 --> 00:02:45.621
One of them was sleep deprived,
the other of whom was drunk,

00:02:45.621 --> 00:02:49.785
drunk to the legal driving limit,
so 0.08% of blood alcohol.

00:02:49.785 --> 00:02:54.769
And he gave both of these groups
a concentration test to assess cognitive

00:02:54.769 --> 00:03:00.000
performance, what he found was that
have to just 19 hours of being awake,

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.741
19 hours, the sleep deprived group
was as cognitively impaired,

00:03:04.741 --> 00:03:07.296
as the group that was legally drunk.

00:03:07.296 --> 00:03:11.322
So think about what that means, imagine
you wake up on a Friday at 7 AM and

00:03:11.322 --> 00:03:15.161
you go to work, and you spend your
day at work, then maybe you go out.

00:03:15.161 --> 00:03:19.697
On the evening, on a date or
with friends, typical Friday, and

00:03:19.697 --> 00:03:24.569
even if you drink no alcohol whatsoever,
by the time you're driving

00:03:24.569 --> 00:03:29.453
back home at 2 in the morning,
you're essentially a drunk driver.

00:03:29.453 --> 00:03:34.719
But you might say, wait I really do get
five or six hours of sleep per night,

00:03:34.719 --> 00:03:39.483
the fact that I just got six hours of
sleep over the past few nights and

00:03:39.483 --> 00:03:43.102
I feel great, I feel fantastic,
am I delusional?

00:03:43.102 --> 00:03:45.205
And the answer is, yes.

00:03:45.205 --> 00:03:50.617
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yes, you are.

00:03:50.617 --> 00:03:54.145
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Dr. Dinges experiment had a twist and

00:03:54.145 --> 00:03:58.718
when he asked the sleep deprived group
how cognitively impaired they felt,

00:03:58.718 --> 00:04:02.731
they significantly underestimated
their degree of disability.

00:04:02.731 --> 00:04:05.929
In fact,
their self assessment was a terrible,

00:04:05.929 --> 00:04:09.442
miserable predictor of just
how bad they performed.

00:04:09.442 --> 00:04:13.838
Another major problem tied to this is
something called baseline resetting.

00:04:13.838 --> 00:04:18.550
And it's been shown that after months or
years of chronic sleep deprivation, people

00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:22.948
actually acclimate to their impaired
performance and reduced energy levels.

00:04:22.948 --> 00:04:27.773
Never even realizing that they're
failing to maximize the potential of

00:04:27.773 --> 00:04:28.809
mind and body.

00:04:28.809 --> 00:04:33.399
Epidemiological studies suggest
that millions of people spend

00:04:33.399 --> 00:04:36.206
years of their lives living like this.

00:04:36.206 --> 00:04:42.034
So bottom line is, we don't know how sleep
deprived we are when we're sleep deprived,

00:04:42.034 --> 00:04:44.732
and that's the scariest part of it all.

00:04:44.732 --> 00:04:49.467
Now onto our second myth, we've all
probably set at some points in our lives,

00:04:49.467 --> 00:04:51.996
I'll just sleep it off over the weekend.

00:04:51.996 --> 00:04:56.261
And the research shows that just isn't
the case our brains are incapable of that.

00:04:56.261 --> 00:04:59.581
So back to Dr. Dinges study,
after the initial test,

00:04:59.581 --> 00:05:04.142
he took the sleep deprived group and
gave them three full recovery nights.

00:05:04.142 --> 00:05:08.615
So three full nights of sleep,
then tested them again,

00:05:08.615 --> 00:05:12.721
what do you think happened
the second time around?

00:05:12.721 --> 00:05:16.054
Turns out that even after
three full recovery nights,

00:05:16.054 --> 00:05:20.190
performance still did not return
to the original baseline levels.

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:23.066
Sleep just doesn't work like that,

00:05:23.066 --> 00:05:28.723
fact we now know that lack of sleep
builds up and compounds over time, and

00:05:28.723 --> 00:05:34.976
research even links to some of the most
feared diseases in the developed world.

00:05:34.976 --> 00:05:37.318
One of them is Alzheimers.

00:05:37.318 --> 00:05:41.455
Alzheimer's is triggered by
a metabolic toxin called beta amyloid,

00:05:41.455 --> 00:05:45.590
which builds up naturally in our
brains over the course of the day, and

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:47.850
sleep offers a cleansing mechanism.

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:51.980
And so the less sleep you're
having across your lifespan,

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:57.855
the more of that toxic protein is building
up, night after night, year after year.

00:05:57.855 --> 00:06:02.571
So when sufficient sleep is now considered
one of the most significant lifestyle

00:06:02.571 --> 00:06:06.611
factors, determining whether or
not you'll develop the disease.

00:06:06.611 --> 00:06:11.519
And for that reason, Dr.
Walker from Berkeley goes as far

00:06:11.519 --> 00:06:16.527
as to call wakefulness,
a state of low level brain damage,

00:06:16.527 --> 00:06:20.352
whereas sleep offers a repertory function.

00:06:20.352 --> 00:06:23.804
Okay so sleep matters,
I hope you all agree with me on that.

00:06:23.804 --> 00:06:25.653
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So what's the odd-shot?

00:06:25.653 --> 00:06:28.791
What can we do to help
combat sleep machismo and

00:06:28.791 --> 00:06:31.131
address the sleepless epidemic?

00:06:31.131 --> 00:06:35.530
So first of at an individual level, there
is a tonne of research and literature out

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:40.022
there on insomnia and how to improve your
sleep, so I won't delve too much into it.

00:06:40.022 --> 00:06:43.352
All I'll say is that by far
the best thing you can do for

00:06:43.352 --> 00:06:46.917
your sleep is simply give
yourself an eight hour window.

00:06:46.917 --> 00:06:50.183
No, it takes a meaningful
lifestyle change, but

00:06:50.183 --> 00:06:53.371
it's totally worth it,
on the business level,

00:06:53.371 --> 00:06:58.304
despite what we now know about sleep,
sleep machismo remains pervasive.

00:06:58.304 --> 00:07:03.552
In fact, lack of sleep is often
even boasted as a badge of honor.

00:07:03.552 --> 00:07:08.041
As an example, in my former job as
a consultant one of the lead partners in

00:07:08.041 --> 00:07:12.175
the firm loved to have sleep as
one of his work/life balance KPIs.

00:07:12.175 --> 00:07:18.933
And his aspirational metric to gauge
how much sleep he was getting was,

00:07:18.933 --> 00:07:25.575
having no less than four and a half
hours of sleep per night on average,

00:07:25.575 --> 00:07:30.548
four and a half hours, and
that was the aspiration.

00:07:30.548 --> 00:07:35.284
Scores on mindset trickle down to the rest
of the team and the broader firm and

00:07:35.284 --> 00:07:37.883
reinforce the stigmatization of sleep.

00:07:37.883 --> 00:07:42.450
So from those overnight red eye flights
to the always on expectation and

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:47.779
the indefinite work schedules that often
drag beyond midnight, I believe that's

00:07:47.779 --> 00:07:52.904
the merits a radical rethink of policies
and priorities in the business world.

00:07:52.904 --> 00:07:57.001
Because awareness drives action and
change has to come from the top.

00:08:00.384 --> 00:08:04.446
We've all heard the old maxim,
you can sleep when you're dead.

00:08:06.124 --> 00:08:09.494
Let me tell you that is,
mortally unwise advice.

00:08:09.494 --> 00:08:14.437
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We now know that the shorter you sleep,

00:08:14.437 --> 00:08:16.269
the shorter you live, and

00:08:16.269 --> 00:08:21.862
the quality of that now shorter life will
be much worse, sleep is not a paradox.

00:08:21.862 --> 00:08:26.802
It is not a luxury reserved for
those of us without busy lives,

00:08:26.802 --> 00:08:30.707
it is a biological necessity and
it is critical.

00:08:30.707 --> 00:08:36.897
I sincerely hope that you'll
reflect on some of these learnings,

00:08:36.897 --> 00:08:40.993
and if you don't, please just sleep on it.

00:08:40.993 --> 00:08:42.169
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:08:42.169 --> 00:08:44.186
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:44.186 --> 00:08:48.207
[MUSIC]

